# Module comments (decision + why wiring)

"Module" = a **logical section inside a source file**, not a folder name.

Hand-written source keeps code **visually ordered**: blank line between sections, and a short comment that is **technical and didactic** — enough to teach the wiring, not an essay.

## 0. File-level decision (required on non-trivial files)

After the author signature (see `author-js-ts-file-signature.mdc` / `author-html-css-file-signature.mdc`), open with a short **DECISION** block in Spanish (no diacritics):

1. **Objetivo** — que resuelve este archivo
2. **Particion** — como se parto el problema y como se conectan las piezas
3. **Definiciones** — tokens, tipos, constantes o contratos que cargan el diseno (solo los que importan)

Do **not** fill the decision with process talk (e.g. "no JS", "CSS alcanza", "editable solo desde codigo") unless that constraint is the actual product goal.

```ts
//Mariano Montini ('bosque', 'bosquestudio')

/*
  DECISION
  Objetivo: <que hay que lograr>
  Particion: <ejes / modulos en que se parto y como se conectan>
  Definiciones: <tokens | tipos | constantes que cargan el diseno>
*/
```

CSS / HTML use the same content with `/* ... */` or `<!-- ... -->`.

Skip the decision block only for trivial one-liners or pure re-exports.

## 1. What needs a section comment

Comment every coherent block:

| Section kind | Examples |
|---|---|
| Parameter / props / options groups | `type XProps`, config constants |
| Schema / field groups | Zod / DTO fields |
| State groups | Related `useState` / refs |
| Function blocks | Exported functions, handlers, hook callbacks |
| Return / public API groups | Hook `return { ... }` |
| JSX / markup regions (non-trivial) | Form fields, list, actions |
| CSS rule groups | Tokens shell, variants, motion, layout chrome |

## 2. What each module comment must say

Above each section, explain **wiring**, not narration — **always in Spanish without diacritics**:

- Why this block exists in the chain (what it connects to)
- Why these definitions / tokens / instructions / logic are the ones used

Tone: technical, slightly didactic, human — **not** robotic checklists (`Por que:`, `Why:`, `Note:`). No paraphrasing the code line-by-line. No filler.

Prefer **1–3 short lines**. Dense is fine; long is not.

### JS / TS format

```ts
// Composicion del preview - srcdoc deja el HTML editable y el CSS como fuente hermana;
// inyectar <style> antes de </head> evita un segundo fetch en cada tecla.
function buildSrcdoc(htmlText, cssText) {
  // ...
}
```

### CSS format

```css
/*
  Presets hard-stop - from%/to% fija bandas planas para que la cantidad de franjas sea un eje;
  --c* queda en el tema asi paleta y posterizado no se duplican en reglas aparte.
*/
.grad-surface--stops-5 { /* ... */ }
```

## 3. Visual separation

- One blank line between sections; do not glue unrelated blocks
- Do not leave a wall of uncommented functions / rule groups
- Group related params under one commented block when that clarifies the contract
- **Comment language (mandatory): Spanish without diacritics** — avoid `á é í ó ú ü ñ` and punctuation like `… – ≈` so files stay ASCII-safe. Applies to TS/JS/HTML/CSS module comments and the file-level DECISION block. Keep identifiers, APIs, and code tokens in their real names (`srcdoc`, `--grad-speed`, `fetch`, etc.)

## 4. Folder modularity (still required)

Presentation / features-services / infrastructure / contracts stay in separate folders/files. Section comments document **blocks inside** those files.

## 5. Skip list

Skip `node_modules/`, `.next/`, `dist/`, `build/`, `coverage/`, generated stubs, and files the toolchain owns/overwrites.
